    "title": "Pandas — From Beginner to Professional",
    "description": "A visual-first, practice-led Pandas course following the attached 52-page curriculum: foundations, Series and DataFrames, inspection, selection, type conversion, cleaning, transformation, text and categorical data, statistics, GroupBy, joins, reshaping, I/O, time series, visualization, MultiIndex, performance, interoperability, testing, production workflows, and specialist topics.",
    "category": "Python / Data Science",
    "level": "Beginner to Advanced",
    "sort_order": 34,
    "version_scope": "Course reference target: official stable Pandas 3.0.6 documentation checked 2026-10-13. Version-sensitive lessons cover Pandas 3.0 default string dtype, Copy-on-Write, datetime resolution and removed/deprecated APIs. Runtime validation in this authoring environment used Pandas 2.2.3; examples specifically tied to 3.x remain marked for target-version review before publication.",
    "audience": [
      "Python learners",
      "students",
      "data analysts",
      "scientific programmers",
      "aspiring machine-learning developers",
      "advanced Python developers"
    ],
    "prerequisites": [
      "Basic Python syntax, lists/dictionaries, functions, importing modules, and basic statistical terms. NumPy is helpful but not required for the initial modules."
    ],
    "estimated_workload_hours": 65,
    "lesson_count": 216,
    "module_count": 26,
    "learning_outcomes": [
      "Install and configure Pandas in a repeatable Python environment.",
      "Create, inspect, select, filter, sort, convert, and modify Series and DataFrames.",
      "Import structured data, clean inconsistent records, handle missing/duplicate data, and validate schemas.",
      "Use vectorized calculations, statistics, grouping, joins, and reshaping to answer analytical questions.",
      "Work with text, categories, dates, time series, visualizations, and interoperable data formats.",
      "Create and test reliable analysis pipelines, understand performance trade-offs, and maintain version compatibility.",
      "Deliver end-to-end analysis projects with reproducible inputs, quality controls, tests, and documented limitations."
    ],
